BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Breaking the Mold: Careers for the Common Good - Dinner and Keynot
 e Address
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261022T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261022T203000
DTSTAMP:20261008T170805Z
UID:dartmouth-82337@free-food-dartmouth
SEQUENCE:0
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Original event page(s):\nhttps://home.dartmouth.edu/events/eve
 nt?event=82337\nhttps://dartmouthgroups.dartmouth.edu/rsvp_boot?id=1631072
 \n\nDescription:\nJoin us on October 22nd for the inaugural event of this 
 year's Breaking the Mold. Breaking the Mold is a 2-day conference sponsore
 d by the Dartmouth Center for Social Impact (DCSI) that seeks to provide i
 nformation and alumni networking opportunities for students interested in 
 exploring or pursuing careers for the common good.\nDuring this dinner and
  keynote event\, participants will have the opportunity to interact with d
 istinguished alumni and learn about what working towards the common good m
 eans to them.\nA keynote will be delivered by Jim Bildner\, Class of 1975.
  Jim is a nationally recognized lecturer\, panelist\, speaker\, and though
 t leader on nonprofit organizations\, social enterprise\, capitalization\,
  and the institutional role of philanthropy in solving complex societal is
 sues. He is the CEO of the Draper Richards Kaplan (DRK) Foundation\, a lea
 ding global venture philanthropy firm that finds\, funds\, and supports so
 cial impact organizations working to address some of society’s most pres
 sing social and environmental challenges. Jim sits on numerous boards and 
 is also an Adjunct Faculty member at the Harvard Kennedy School. He will s
 peak to his newest book\, A Matter of Hope: Lessons Learned from Leaders\,
  Changemakers\, and Visionaries on Making a Difference in the World\, publ
 ished in September 2026. A Matter of Hope\, already a national best seller
 \, currently sits as #13 on USA Today's list of Top 150 books!\nA buffet d
 inner will be served for all registered participants. Vegetarian\, vegan\,
  and gluten-free options will be available.\nThe required dress code is Bu
 siness Casual.\nIf you RSVP but become unable attend\, please email Sai Wa
 ters at sai.waters@dartmouth.edu by Monday\, October 19th so that we can o
 ffer your seat to a student on the waitlist. Students who do not cancel by
  the deadline and do not attend the event will be charged $50 to their DAS
 H account to cover the cost of catering.\nSlots are limited and registrati
 on will be accepted on a first come first serve basis. As this is a career
  focused event\, so priority for registration will be given to seniors.\n-
 --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 -------------------------------------------------\nIn addition to this eve
 nt\, there will be a series of lunch panels around specific social impact 
 themes on Friday\, October 23rd. See additional event postings for details
 .\nVisit DCSI's website for the latest updates and registration informatio
 n along with details about alumni attendees as they become available.\nTha
 nk you to our campus partners\, including the Center for Career Design (CC
 D)\, Tuck School of Business\, the Education faculty\, and Social Entrepre
 neurship + Innovation at Dartmouth (SEID)\, for supporting this initiative
 !\nFood Provided (3-course dinner (salad\, entree\, dessert) with vegetari
 an\, vegan\, and gluten-free options available. )\n\nSponsor / organizer: 
 Dartmouth Center for Social Impact\n\nAudience: Public\n\nSource: Dartmout
 h\, Dartmouth Groups\n\nWhy this was included: Free Food category\; explic
 it food-service wording\; food or meal keyword in title\n\nFood availabili
 ty is inferred from the source listing\; verify details before attending.
